{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779484596,"nonce":"WhFlLBlXfFaDR9CwF89Ck5/NXOE=","message":"## Cross-reference: core graph recurrence and nested iteration semantics\n\nNew core Feedbax issues from the recurrence/state review:\n\n- `f93db6a` — Core graph recurrence contract and cycle initializers. This tracks explicit backend/provider semantics for `instant` vs `recurrent` wires, recurrent initial values, validation of same-step cycles, and stable cycle-cut representation. It preserves the current backend split: port wires are real per-step dataflow, while `StateIndex` is owned component state.\n- `56dfd97` — Long-term nested subgraph inner-iteration semantics. This is deferred future design for intentional inner rollouts, fixed-count refinements, multirate dynamics, or solver-like loops inside one parent graph step.\n\nStudio companion issue: `7f02d30` tracks recurrent-edge authoring/rendering and lightweight stateful-node inspection.\n\nDesign stance: ordinary embedded subgraphs should remain single-step components from the parent graph's perspective. Internal recurrent cuts are fine when they advance on the same timestep as the parent. If a subgraph intentionally runs additional inner timesteps, that needs an explicit graph/schema representation rather than being hidden behind a normal subgraph node.","files":null}]}